      "title": "Marching Band Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-04\n\nLocation: AVE MARIA\n\nJob Title:  Marching Band Visual & Percussion Instructor Employee Class:  Seasonal-Athletics Department:  Athletics – Marching Band FLSA Status:  Hourly Reports To:  Marching Band Director   Effective:   PRIMARY PURPOSE: The Marching Band Visual & Percussion Instructor is a key member of the marching band instructional team, supporting both visual execution and percussion instruction. This position plays a vital role in the development of the drumline and overall field performance, working closely with the Marching Band Director, student leadership, and band members to ensure an exceptional performance and educational experience. Responsibilities include teaching and refining marching and playing techniques, mentoring student leaders, leading sectionals and rehearsals, and supporting performance logistics. Instruction and Teaching: Teach and refine marching techniques including posture, stride, alignment, and visual precision. Instruct the drumline in playing technique, marching technique, and performance readiness. Provide individualized feedback to students to improve musical and visual performance. Conduct field rehearsals, sectionals, and full ensemble rehearsals for both visual and percussion execution. Attend rehearsals and performances for quality control and fine-tuning. Drill & Visual Implementation: Assist with teaching and cleaning field drill, including accurate placement and movement of performers. Utilize technology (e.g., drone footage, Pyware, rehearsal software) to enhance visual performance. Lead on-field warm-ups and coordinate pre-show routines. Leadership Development: Mentor student leaders, including drum majors and section leaders, to enhance their instructional and leadership capabilities. Facilitate workshops to build leadership, instructional skills, and team culture. Coordination and Logistics: Assist in planning and execution of band camp, rehearsals, and performances. Ensure appropriate use of field equipment including field markers, metronomes, and sound systems. Support organization and transportation of equipment for performances and travel. Student Support & Program Promotion Monitor academic, social, and personal development of band members. Help with student recruitment and evaluation. Assist in building relationships with alumni, donors, and the community to promote the band program. Collaboration and Communication: Work closely with the Director of Bands to align field activities with the program’s artistic and educational goals. Maintain clear and consistent communication with student leadership and staff regarding rehearsal schedules, expectations, and adjustments. EDUCATION: Bachelor’s degree in Music, Music Education, or a related field required. Significant experience in the marching arts may suffice. Master’s degree a plus. EXPERIENCE: Previous experience in teaching marching techniques and drill design/implementation at the high school or collegiate level required. Experience in drumline instruction, visual design, or drill implementation strongly preferred. Familiarity with marching band drill design software (Pyware) and field technology. Experience as a performer or staff member in a collegiate marching band or drum corps. Proven success in mentoring student leaders and fostering a positive band culture. CERTIFICATION:  N/A PHYSICAL SKILLS AND ABILITIES: Ability to demonstrate marching and playing techniques, including prolonged standing and movement on the field. Capacity to lift and transport field equipment as needed. WORKING CONDITIONS: Work is performed indoors and outdoors with potential for outdoor exposure to high temperatures, and safety and health hazards related to band instruction. Evening and weekend work is required during marching season. Outdoor rehearsals in varying weather conditions. Travel for performances and recruiting events as necessary.   The responsibilities and requirements listed above are not an exhaustive list of all that will be expected of this position; however, it is met to identify the general criteria for this position.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=70cb71c1-96f8-4956-8cce-ce625b0b2943&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9205232565928_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Faculty Position-Asst. Professor of History",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\n\nLocation: AVE MARIA\n\nThe History Department at Ave Maria University invites applications for a full-time Assistant Professor of History position to start in the 2027-2028 academic year. Preference will be given to: Candidates with expertise in a subfield of American history. Candidates who have earned a doctoral degree in History or a closely related field. Candidates with some teaching experience and a promising research agenda. Candidates equipped to contribute to the University’s Catholic identity and mission. Applicants must be authorized to work in the United States for full consideration. The following documents must be submitted: - Cover Letter - Curriculum Vitae, including a list of publications - Graduate and Undergraduate University Transcripts (unofficial transcripts are acceptable initially, official transcripts to be furnished upon request) - Teaching Philosophy as it Relates to the Apostolic Constitution Ex Corde Ecclesiae - Research Statement: Research in progress and further interests - Three Letters of Recommendation -Optional supporting materials: Sample syllabi from courses taught, course evaluations Review of applications will begin on November 1 st , 2026 and will continue until the position is filled. Rank and salary will be commensurate with the candidate’s credentials. Ave Maria University is a private Catholic university offering over 30 majors, including master’s and doctoral degrees. A Newman Guide School faithful to the Catholic Church’s Magisterium, our university provides one of the strongest classical liberal arts curricula in the country, as well as exceptional opportunities for specialized study in the sciences and humanities. The campus is located in sunny Southwest Florida, in the vibrant and growing town of Ave Maria, Florida. Contact Information Dr. Paul Baxa Chair, Department of History Email: Paul.Baxa@avemaria.edu",
      "department": ""
    },
